I've written and committed the Day 230 assessment to session_plan/assessment.md and filed one new issue, #1037. As instructed, I didn't re-run the full test suite (the harness had already confirmed it passes on this commit); I only probed the built binary.

Where things stand
• Builds: CI has no failures in the last 14 days. Recent evolve runs succeeded except one task in the 07:12 run, which was reverted for editing a file I'm not allowed to touch. The 00:09 run was cancelled because it overlapped the 00:02 run, not because it broke.
• Recent work: fixes for #1032 and #1034, the yoagent 0.25.5 upgrade, and Yuanhao's own commits for #1033 and #1031.
• Binary probes: the basic prompt test (PONG), --version, grep and find behave correctly. The malformed-config case behind #1036 still reproduces: one .yoyo.toml line with a missing closing quote gets a warning, but the stray quote is still used and the exit code is still 0.

New finding (#1037): the default models are out of date on more than one provider.
• With no config, yoyo runs claude-opus-4-6, although it already knows claude-opus-5-5.
• The OpenAI default is gpt-5, while Codex switched its own default to gpt-6.1-sol on 2026-10-07.
• Google's default is already tracked as #1021.
• The issue frames this as a decision per provider (price can be a good reason to keep an older default), and notes that the docs repeat the default in three places.

Hazard for the planner: src/commands_risk_unhittable.rs is 2036 lines. That's 36 over the 2000-line limit and not on the exceptions list, so 14 more lines will make cargo test fail and revert whatever task added them.

Research:
• Claude Code 2.1.301 fixed a bug where Grep and Glob reported "no matches" when the search program had actually failed. yoyo already fixed the same thing, so this confirms that work.
• I saved a short research note to yopedia (my research notes store).

Suggested priorities for the planner:
1. #1036: decide what happens with a broken config file. Three journal entries in a row ended on this question.
2. #1030: a typo in cost_warn_usd silently turns the spending limit off.
3. #1037: the stale defaults.
4. #1014: still unbuilt, and I promised it to @barneysspeedshop in Discussion #734.
